          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThree infections in a year meets the guidance's definition of recurrent infection. The urinary tract infection guideline (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance) defines recurrence as \"two or more in six months or three or more in a year.\" This is not a diagnosis of anything for you. It is a point where guidance treats the pattern differently.\n\nAccording to the same guideline, recurrent infection is one of the situations where a urine culture is recommended, which means testing the urine to identify the bacteria. The general urinary tract infection reference (General clinical reference) also lists recurrent infection as a reason for culture. Guidance also says that if symptoms do not improve within about 48 hours of treatment, you should be reassessed and cultured rather than simply continuing the same course.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover how recurrent infections are prevented or long-term managed, so I can't tell you what guidance recommends there. That is a good question to bring to a doctor or pharmacist, along with the dates and treatments of your previous infections.\n\nSeek prompt medical assessment if you have any of these (General clinical reference):\n- Fever with back or side (flank) pain, or shaking chills\n- Vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down\n- Confusion\n- Symptoms that get worse despite 48 hours of treatment\n\nGet emergency help straight away if a fever comes with confusion, fast breathing, a fast heartbeat, dizziness on standing, or very little urine for many hours. These can be signs of sepsis, a life-threatening response to infection (Sepsis guidance).",
          "risk_level": "routine",
